            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the cross product of two 3-D vectors: a third vector at right angles to both.\n\nIts direction is perpendicular to the plane the two inputs lie in, and its length is the area of the parallelogram they span - largest when they are at right angles, 0 when they are parallel (then there is no single plane, and no perpendicular). Order matters: in 1 x in 2 points the opposite way from in 2 x in 1 (the right-hand rule: fingers from in 1 towards in 2, thumb along the result).\n\nReach for it to find a surface normal from two edges of a triangle, the axis to turn about to get from one direction to another, or which way is \"up\" given a forward and a sideways direction.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.cross\n(no arguments)\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe first vector, 3 numbers (a list is turned into an array). Receiving it calculates and sends.\n\nin 2:\nThe second vector. It is only stored, so it must arrive BEFORE in 1;\nnothing is sent until it has.\nSets of vectors work row by row: N rows of 3 with N rows of 3 (or with one vector of 3) give N cross products. Two 2-D vectors give a single number (the z of the result) and numpy warns that this form is going away. Other lengths are an error: it is printed and nothing is sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncross product:\nThe perpendicular vector, 3 numbers (N rows of 3 for sets).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\na and b start as the x and y directions, [1 0 0] and [0 1 0]; their cross product is [0 0 1], the z direction. Drag any of their numbers. The two np.dot nodes check the result against each input, and both stay at 0 - the cross product is always at right angles to a and to b.\nnp.linalg.norm gives its length: 1 for x and y, smaller as you swing b towards a, 0 when they are parallel.\nWiring: b goes through 't bang array' - stored first, then the bang makes a re-send - and a's 't array array' stores a in the first check before the cross product is worked out.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.dot measures how much two vectors agree; the cross product is what they do not share. np.linalg.norm gives the cross product's length (the area). np.linalg.det of the 3 x 3 matrix with rows a, b, c is the volume they span - the dot of c with a x b. np.outer is unrelated despite the name.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 908,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the cross product of two 3-D vectors: a third vector at right angles to both.\n\nIts direction is perpendicular to the plane the two inputs lie in, and its length is the area of the parallelogram they span - largest when they are at right angles, 0 when they are parallel (then there is no single plane, and no perpendicular). Order matters: in 1 x in 2 points the opposite way from in 2 x in 1 (the right-hand rule: fingers from in 1 towards in 2, thumb along the result).\n\nReach for it to find a surface normal from two edges of a triangle, the axis to turn about to get from one direction to another, or which way is \"up\" given a forward and a sideways direction.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.cross\n(no arguments)\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin 1:\nThe first vector, 3 numbers (a list is turned into an array). Receiving it calculates and sends.\n\nin 2:\nThe second vector. It is only stored, so it must arrive BEFORE in 1;\nnothing is sent until it has.\nSets of vectors work row by row: N rows of 3 with N rows of 3 (or with one vector of 3) give N cross products. Two 2-D vectors give a single number (the z of the result) and numpy warns that this form is going away. Other lengths are an error: it is printed and nothing is sent.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncross product:\nThe perpendicular vector, 3 numbers (N rows of 3 for sets).\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\na and b start as the x and y directions, [1 0 0] and [0 1 0]; their cross product is [0 0 1], the z direction. Drag any of their numbers. The two np.dot nodes check the result against each input, and both stay at 0 - the cross product is always at right angles to a and to b.\nnp.linalg.norm gives its length: 1 for x and y, smaller as you swing b towards a, 0 when they are parallel.\nWiring: b goes through 't bang array' - stored first, then the bang makes a re-send - and a's 't array array' stores a in the first check before the cross product is worked out.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.dot measures how much two vectors agree; the cross product is what they do not share. np.linalg.norm gives the cross product's length (the area). np.linalg.det of the 3 x 3 matrix with rows a, b, c is the volume they span - the dot of c with a x b. np.outer is unrelated despite the name."
        },
